---
description: Write with as little noise as possible. Persist actual payloads; no remaps or defensive guards.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Little noise

Smallest code that does the job. Persist and show the payload as it is. `cleanDeep` already drops null/empty, so do not re-guard those fields.

- No remapping: do not rename, format, title-case, invent `status`/`name`, or collapse rows. Spread the source object. Extra keys only when storage requires them (`createdBy`; `pagoId` because Firestore overwrites `id`)
- No defensive code: no try/catch unless recovering (401 re-login, mark a job failed); no `== null` continues; no fallbacks for required fields
- `|| {}` / `|| []` only at a destructure. `billsList || []` is the one restore `cleanDeep` needs when it strips an empty array off an object
- Trust the payload. If the field is there, use it. If it was empty, it is already gone

```js
// noisy
for (const bill of bills || []) {
  const { id: pagoId } = bill || {}
  if (pagoId == null) continue
  await invoiceService.update(hash, {
    name: formatDate(bill.dueDate),
    status: "due",
    dueDate: dayjs(bill.dueDate).format("YYYY-MM-DD"),
    createdBy
  })
}

// quiet
for (const bill of bills) {
  const { id: pagoId } = bill
  await invoiceService.update(objectHash({ pagoId: String(pagoId) }), {
    ...bill,
    pagoId,
    createdBy
  })
}
```
